The additional dataset I found was contained in the RefSeq database. 

* What sort of data is it? What sort of information does it offer us?
It contains different types of data, including DNA and mRNA data. It adds additional annotaions regarding the gene, including the location of different relevant sites such as Poly-A site, CDS, and others depending on the gene. 
An example of a dataset annotated from RefSeq is the dataset "Bauhinia variegata isolate BV-YZ2020 chromosome 14, whole genome shotgun sequence"

* When and where was it published? Was it published?
RefSeq is supported by the NCBI. This dataset draws on the publication, "Chromosomal-level genome assembly of the orchid tree Bauhiniavariegata (Leguminosae; Cercidoideae) supports the allotetraploid origin hypothesis of Bauhinia" by Zhong et al., published in DNA Research.

* Is this annotation set updated regularly or is it a static source?
It is a static source, and was last updated June 2022. The general RefSeq database is continually updated as submissions are submitted to the database. 

* Where can I find this data? (link to the download web address or ftp site or publication where it can be found)
The dataset can be found here: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nuccore/JAKRYI020000014.1.

* How is the data formatted and released? Does it exist in some sort of standard file format?
It is released as text on the web server, but it is primarily meant to be downloaded as a FASTA file. 

*  What identifiers are associated with these annotations?
It examines chromosome 14 and has a custom identifier format. It displays the gene, mRNA, and CDS positions for each gene on the chromosome. 
